  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1012 PM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nOur big heavy rain event is expected to peak over the next 6-12 \nhours. All the ingredients are present for multiple rounds of \nconvection tonight through Wed AM with storm motion generally from \nsouth to north. Tropical moisture is being pulled northward (0z RAOB \nPWs recorded 1.29\") ahead of an upper trough off the West Coast, \nwith that pattern holding steady through Wed. For tonight, we'll be \nunderneath the favorable right entrance region of a jet streak \nthat's over the Central Rockies. Additionally, vort maxes/embedded \nshortwaves pushing in from Chihuahua would provide more forcing for \nstorm development.\n\nThe HREF and REFS have been very consistent over the past 24 hours \nwith a corridor of heavy rainfall developing by midnight into \nsouthern Luna and Dona Ana Counties. This fetch of moisture and \nconvection is modeled to expand northward through Dona Ana and \nSierra Counties early Wed AM. The heaviest rain should mostly avoid \nEl Paso, but the sharp QPF gradient in the CAMs is too close for \ncomfort. Probability matched mean data focuses the heaviest rainfall \nover central Dona Ana through SE Sierra Counties. Midnight-6am is \nthe most likely timeframe to see flash flooding in these areas. \nRainfall amounts are very impressive for Dona Ana and Sierra with \nPMM showing 2-3\" in the Las Cruces vicinity and slightly lower \namounts towards T or C (1-2\"). High-end rainfall shows 4\" or more in \nisolated areas.\n\nWith all that being said, scattered to numerous instances of flash \nflooding are expected early Wed AM with isolated considerable or \neven catastrophic flooding. Arroyos, sensitive canyons, burn scars \n(later Wed), and urban areas are most vulnerable. Even rises on the \nRio Grande can be seen with minor flood stage not out of the \nquestion if the higher end rainfall comes to pass. Most of this rain \nwill eventually end up in the Rio Grande or Sierra Co reservoirs. \nAreas along and W of the Divide and E of the 54 corridor are \nforecast to avoid the worst of the rain through Wed AM with more \nisolated flash flooding. \n\nThe heavy rain threat shifts NE during the day Wed with a \nshortwave/vort max, but scattered to widespread storms are still \nforecast. Less confidence in location and rain amounts Wed PM, but \ngenerally favoring Sierra and Otero Counties. All areas have at \nleast a low risk of flooding through Wed night, decreasing out west. \nAdditional rainfall of 2\" is possible in isolated spots for Wed PM. \nFor Thu, the flood threat focuses out east as drier air filters in \nwest of the RGV. PWs remain at least 50% above normal east of the \nRGV with a general low risk of flooding and more scattered \nconvection. There's a concerning QPF bulls-eye over the Salt burn \nscar on the 18z REFS Thu afternoon, so that's something to keep an \neye on.\n\nMoisture levels and storm chances continue to diminish into the \nweekend with mostly isolated storm coverage by Sat and PWs back to \nnear normal. Area mtns and eastern areas will be favored for storms. \nSubtropical moisture looks to return early next week from the \nPacific, possibly associated with a tropical system. PWs should \nrebound to above normal with low-medium storm chances area-wide to \nend the period.\n\nTemps will be well below normal for Wed (lowland highs in the 70s) \ndue to the extensive cloud cover. We'll gradually warm up to near \nnormal for the weekend. Winds will be light outside of any gusty \noutflows. Any storms that develop shouldn't produce gusts much more \nthan 40 mph since we're so saturated.",
